\ A call to an `inline` word is replaced at definition time by a copy of that word's body. The
\ marker is a hint, so every word below computes what it would have computed without it.
use "testing" ;

\ The stored body, rendered. Expansion is invisible to a program's output, so this is what a test
\ has to look at to see that it happened at all.
body-of: ( sym -- string ) [ >word-info body>> inspect ] ;

doubled: ( n -- n ) const inline [ 2 * ] ;
quadruple: ( n -- n ) [ doubled doubled ] ;

"a marked word's body replaces the call" test-section

doubled: body-of "[ 2 * ]" "the marked word keeps its own body" assert=
quadruple: body-of "[ 2 * 2 * ]" "each call site carries a copy" assert=
21 quadruple 84 "and the word still computes what it did" assert=

"expansion runs at each definition, so a chain arrives flat" test-section

add-one:  ( n -- n ) const inline [ 1 + ] ;
add-two:  ( n -- n ) const inline [ add-one add-one ] ;
add-four: ( n -- n ) [ add-two add-two ] ;

add-two: body-of "[ 1 + 1 + ]" "the middle word is expanded when it is defined" assert=
add-four: body-of "[ 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + ]" "so the outer one never re-walks the chain" assert=
10 add-four 14 "and the chain still computes what it did" assert=

"a call that would re-enter an open body stays a call" test-section

\ `countdown` names itself. Its own definition expands nothing, because the name resolves to
\ nothing while the definition is still being built.
countdown: ( n -- n ) const inline [ dup 0 > [ 1 - countdown ] when ] ;
drive: ( n -- n ) [ countdown ] ;

countdown: body-of "[ dup 0 > [ 1 - countdown ] when ]" "a self-call is left alone" assert=
drive: body-of "[ dup 0 > [ 1 - countdown ] when ]" "a caller expands one level and stops" assert=
3 drive 0 "and the recursion still runs to its base case" assert=

"mutual recursion stops at the first re-entry" test-section

ping: ( n -- n ) const inline [ pong ] ;
pong: ( n -- n ) const inline [ 1 + ping ] ;
both: ( n -- n ) [ pong ] ;

\ `pong`'s definition reaches `ping`, whose body names `pong` itself. That is a copy naming the
\ word being defined, so it is declined and `pong` keeps the call it was written with. `both` then
\ expands `pong` and `ping` in turn, and meets the same name with `pong`'s body already open.
\
\ The pair has no base case, so none of the three is callable. What is under test is the shape of
\ the stored body: expansion has to terminate even where the program it expands would not.
pong: body-of "[ 1 + ping ]" "the first of the pair keeps its own call" assert=
both: body-of "[ 1 + pong ]" "and the pair does not alternate forever" assert=

"an unmarked word is left alone" test-section

plain: ( n -- n ) [ 3 * ] ;
calls-plain: ( n -- n ) [ plain ] ;

calls-plain: body-of "[ plain ]" "no marker, no expansion" assert=
2 calls-plain 6 "and it still computes what it did" assert=

"a copy that would name the word being defined stays a call" test-section

\ `bump` names a word that does not exist yet, and `grow` then claims that name. Copying `bump`
\ into `grow` would leave a call to `grow` sitting inside `grow`, in non-tail position. `;` checks
\ a body for a non-tail self-call before the copy happens, so one introduced by the copy would
\ never be checked at all.
bump: ( n -- n ) const inline [ grow ] ;
grow: ( n -- n ) [ 1 + bump ] ;

grow: body-of "[ 1 + bump ]" "the call stands rather than becoming a self-call" assert=

\ A self-call the author wrote is part of the body `;` checked, so it is left as it stands and the
\ marked call beside it still expands.
twice: ( n -- n ) const inline [ 2 * ] ;
walks: ( n -- n ) stack-recursive [ twice walks 0 + ] ;

walks: body-of "[ 2 * walks 0 + ]" "an author's own self-call is untouched" assert=

"a call inside a quotation literal expands too" test-section

\ Where the marker is most wanted. A constant read in a loop or conditional body sits inside a
\ quotation, and reach that stopped at a word's own top level would leave every one of them a call.
width: const inline 256 ;
area: ( -- n ) [ [ width width * ] call ] ;

area: body-of "[ [ 256 256 * ] call ]" "a read one level down is a push" assert=
area 65536 "and it still computes what it did" assert=

\ Two levels, so the descent is not a single step.
volume: ( -- n ) [ [ [ width ] call width * ] call width * ] ;

volume: body-of "[ [ [ 256 ] call 256 * ] call 256 * ]" "and so is a read two levels down" assert=
volume 16777216 "and that still computes too" assert=

\ A quotation holding nothing this pass selects is copied as it stands.
untouched: ( n -- n ) [ [ plain ] call twice ] ;

untouched: body-of "[ [ plain ] call 2 * ]" "a body with nothing to expand is left alone" assert=
2 untouched 12 "and it still computes what it did" assert=
